+\section{Reproduction and Additional Sensitivity Checks}
+
+The local reproduction uses the supplied notebooks and original archives. A second-batch analysis applies only development-derived coefficients, with no fitting or recalibration on second-batch outcomes. Stored second-batch residuals were available, but the original frozen model bytes were not supplied for this audit. The refitted model reproduced those residuals to within $5\times10^{-14}$ percentage points. The recorded model hash and matching numerical behavior do not establish byte identity of reconstructed artifacts. The corrected coverage checker reads the named global-offset field and validates it against the algebra of stored residuals, rather than searching model-summary keys heuristically.
+
+The originally implemented query-box estimator uses empirical coordinate spans. Replacing these with the nominal width $1.1s$ raises occupancy volume by a median 0.1066\% in each batch. This sensitivity does not alter the retained estimator or saved correction. The supplement provides sampling-SD summaries by occupancy under the independent Bernoulli-query approximation.
+
+Boundary replay retains all 60 object IDs and records original acceptance decisions separately from local optimizer status. Figure~\ref{fig:mechanism} distinguishes the 57 originally accepted objects from the three originally rejected fits. Phase and tie-rule checks cover all 550 eligible MitoEM-R objects in 17 configurations (9,350 remeshed object/configuration pairs). All pairs produced nonempty meshes. Their full per-object descriptor, component-count, and Euler-characteristic results are included in the package. These post-audit checks were not prospectively registered and do not replace the original correction-transfer evaluation.
+
+
+The phase sensitivity in Fig.~\ref{fig:phase} shows why the original-grid volume result is procedure-specific. The boundary influence check likewise quantifies sensitivity without deleting a valid observation or redefining the primary acceptance set. All sensitivity outputs are supplied separately from the reconstructed development model.
